course: dictionary
lesson: 4296
title: Grief: a deep sadness
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Grief is a deep, strong sadness, usually after someone or a pet has died or been lost. You can find its meaning in the context: «The sadness after a death is called grief.» The sentence tells you grief is a kind of sadness, and a very big one.
explain: Grief does not always look like crying. «Grief can come out as sadness, anger, tiredness, fear or even laughing; all of these are normal.» So if someone feels cross or sleepy after a loss, the clue is that grief can show up in many different ways.
explain: The context also shows how grief changes over time. «Grief usually gets softer with time» tells you it is strong at first and gentler later. Oscar's grandad says it is «like a bruise that's healing», still there when touched, but no longer sharp all the time.
fact: Grief is a noun, and it is usually uncountable, so we say «full of grief» or «deal with grief», not «a grief».
fact: «Grief» means a deep, strong sadness, especially after a death or a loss.
fact: Grief can come with tears, anger, tiredto ness, fear or numbness, and all of these are normal.
fact: Grief usually gets gentler over months and years. Talking to someone you trust can help.
fact: Playing and laughing during grief does not mean you have forgotten the person.
exercise: «The sadness after a death is called grief.» What is grief? | *A deep, strong sadness | A loud noise | A kind of game | A type of weather
exercise: Oscar's grandad says grief is «like a bruise that's healing». What does this tell you? | *It still hurts when touched, but less sharply over time | It only lasts one day | It stays exactly the same forever | It makes you bruise easily
exercise: Ella says, «Grief can come out as laughing.» Is that true? | *Yes, grief can show in many ways, including laughing | No, grief only ever makes you cry | No, laughing means you have forgotten | Only grown-ups can feel grief
test: Which word from the sentences shows that grief gets easier over time? | sharp | fast | loud | *softer
test: Mia is annoyed and says «Good grief!» What does this phrase show? | *Surprise or annoyance | That she has lost something important | That she is about to go to sleep | Happiness at a party
exam: «Playing and laughing during that time does not mean you have forgotten the person.» What does this sentence tell you? | Laughing means you never loved them | Playing is not allowed during grief | *Laughing after a loss does not mean you have forgotten | Grief always stops when you start to play
